        "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n+6)!$ for any $n\\ge -3?$",
        "worked_solution": "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+6)!=(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,\u22ef\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+6)! & =(n+6)\\cdot \\overset{(n+5)!}{\\overset{\u23de}{(n+5)\\cdot (n+4)\\cdot \\,\u22ef\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+6)\\cdot (n+5)!\\end{aligned}\n$$",
